  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\nShowers will continue to track eastward across mountains, deserts,\nand funky little island bands of showers moving through southwest\nSan Diego County. All showers will end soon and few if any \nshowers should linger after sunrise. Stronger winds especially in \nthe coastal waters, mountains, and deserts will increase this \nmorning and peak around midday before gradually diminishing late \nthis afternoon and evening. Wind gusts in mountains and deserts \nwill be 25-40 mph. Top gusts on mountain ridges and through \nmountain passes will exceed 40 mph, with very isolated gusts \naround 50 mph. It will turn mostly sunny today, but cool and \nbreezy, with highs in upper 60s at lower elevations, lower 70s in \nthe low desert, 50s in the High Desert, and upper 30s to lower 50s\nin the mountains. These max temps are roughly 2-12 degrees below \naverage. Wednesday looks mostly sunny and dry and not windy but \nonly a couple degrees warmer as high pressure does a poor job of \nbuilding. The next weather system with rain and wind arrives \nThursday morning in Orange and western San Bern Counties, \ngradually moving southeast across the region during the day. The \ndetails are becoming better resolved, so we can say with greater \nconfidence our most likely scenario. By the time the storm wraps \nup and moves east early Friday, rainfall should total a little \nover 1 inch in the mountains and areas in the L.A. Basin. Amounts \ndrop off to the south and east with San Diego and Riverside \nCounties receiving less than 0.75 inch. The area around San Diego \nmay get only 0.25 inch. Snow levels will drop to around 6,500 feet\nby the time the precip ends, which should give Big Bear light \nsnowfall, but a few inches could accumulate above 7,500 feet, such\nas on the higher ski slopes. Winds Thursday will be similar to \ntoday's winds, with top gusts in mountains and deserts around \n35-50 mph. Friday and Saturday look fair and dry as the jet stream\npauses the parade of systems crossing the Pacific. By Sunday and \nMonday, things get really uncertain. It could continue dry, but \nmost ensemble solutions bring in a weak system that would bring \nlight rain either Sunday, Sunday night, or Monday. After that, \nthere are some solutions building an anomalously strong ridge over\nthe West that would bring dry and very warm pre- Thanksgiving \nweather. Other solutions keep us in this unsettled, cool, and \nsometimes wet and windy pattern.",
